    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d648">

                <pb facs="0828" n="716" sameAs="frus:frus1918/pg716"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.6363/380</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Tampico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Dawson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tampico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-14T18:00:00-06:41">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 14, 1918, 6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Oil companies are again complaining of threatening attitude Government troops in
                    southern oil fields. Outrage on American workers already charged to them and as
                    result workers of four companies have almost decided to abandon fields.
                    Commanding General Acosta is now in Tampico and promises guarantees, but
                    promises do not inspire confidence. It is generally believed here that border
                    situation is gradually reaching crisis and this new trouble is related to that
                    agitation. Please advise if border trouble is becoming serious. Embassy
                    advised.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dawson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
